pouch boundary-line patch 0011 -- abort() is one trap the kernel names.

History. Phase 6 sub-chunk 16b-gamma-mount-close first overrode musl's
abort() to call _Exit(127): musl's a_crash() (a deliberate NULL store) then
extincted the kernel, so every assert() in a Pouch program took the boot down.
Faults have long since ended only the offending Proc (the snare:* family,
docs/ERRORS.md), and gcpark-b (2026-10-12; operator votes "snare:abort" and
"every note death records a signal") makes abort() a signalled death.

What abort() does. It executes `brk #0xab06`. The immediate is the kernel's
SNARE_ABORT_BRK_IMM (kernel/include/thylacine/notes.h) and is ABI, as the
note name is. The EL0 brk handler (arch/arm64/exception.c, EC_BRK) recognises
it and ends the Proc with the fault note snare:abort:

  - the console prints `user fault: pid=N reason="snare:abort" addr=...
    pc=... lr=... -- terminating Proc`, so lr names abort()'s caller;
  - the kernel records SIGABRT (6) as the Proc's termination signal, so a
    Pouch parent's waitpid reads WIFSIGNALED with WTERMSIG == SIGABRT (patch
    0026 asks for the packed word), a phenotype parent's wait4 reads the same,
    and ut's job-control wait sets $status 134;
  - a native raw wait still reads 1.

Any other brk immediate (a compiler trap, WTFCrash's 0xc471) stays
snare:brk, SIGTRAP. The kernel's comment on the immediate lists the ones it
avoids: 0x1 (clang __builtin_trap), 0x3e8 (gcc), 0xf000 (debuggers),
0x5500-0x55ff (UBSan), 0xc471 (WTF/JSC).

Behavioural delta from upstream abort():
  - No raise(SIGABRT). Pouch runs no SIGABRT handler (signal handlers for the
    snare family are DEFERRED v1.x in docs/ERRORS.md), and the trap is taken
    whatever the signal mask, so abort() cannot be blocked or ignored, as
    POSIX requires of it.
  - No a_crash() and no raise(SIGKILL): the trap replaces both.
  - _Exit(127) stays after the brk: unreachable, the floor should the trap
    ever return.

Threads. A fault in any thread ends the whole Proc (group termination,
I-24), so abort() from any thread of a multi-threaded program is sound. The
old multi-thread hazard of this patch (an exits() with live peer threads
extincted the kernel) was closed by #811.

--- a/src/exit/abort.c
+++ b/src/exit/abort.c
@@ -1,30 +1,15 @@
 #include <stdlib.h>
-#include <signal.h>
-#include "syscall.h"
-#include "pthread_impl.h"
-#include "atomic.h"
-#include "lock.h"
-#include "ksigaction.h"
 
+/* Pouch override (gcpark-b): abort() is one trap the kernel names. The brk
+ * immediate is the kernel's SNARE_ABORT_BRK_IMM (kernel notes.h, ABI): the
+ * EL0 brk handler ends the Proc with the fault note snare:abort, which a
+ * waiting parent reads as WIFSIGNALED with WTERMSIG == SIGABRT, and the
+ * console shows the user-fault line with abort()'s pc and lr. No
+ * raise(SIGABRT): Pouch runs no SIGABRT handler, and the trap is taken
+ * whatever the signal mask. The _Exit(127) is unreachable -- the floor should
+ * the trap ever return. See docs/ERRORS.md "Exit-status semantics". */
 _Noreturn void abort(void)
 {
-	raise(SIGABRT);
-
-	/* If there was a SIGABRT handler installed and it returned, or if
-	 * SIGABRT was blocked or ignored, take an AS-safe lock to prevent
-	 * sigaction from installing a new SIGABRT handler, uninstall any
-	 * handler that may be present, and re-raise the signal to generate
-	 * the default action of abnormal termination. */
-	__block_all_sigs(0);
-	LOCK(__abort_lock);
-	__syscall(SYS_rt_sigaction, SIGABRT,
-		&(struct k_sigaction){.handler = SIG_DFL}, 0, _NSIG/8);
-	__syscall(SYS_tkill, __pthread_self()->tid, SIGABRT);
-	__syscall(SYS_rt_sigprocmask, SIG_UNBLOCK,
-		&(long[_NSIG/(8*sizeof(long))]){1UL<<(SIGABRT-1)}, 0, _NSIG/8);
-
-	/* Beyond this point should be unreachable. */
-	a_crash();
-	raise(SIGKILL);
+	__asm__ __volatile__("brk #0xab06" ::: "memory");
 	_Exit(127);
 }
